<Project>

  <!-- Inherit the repository-wide policy, the way Directory.Build.props does. -->
  <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.targets', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))" />

  <!--
    SPIRV-Cross's native library, put where the loader that asks for it actually looks.

    ⚠ **Shipping a native is not the same as finding it, and Silk.NET's loader is not the
    runtime's.** `Silk.NET.SPIRV.Cross.Native` restores `libspirv-cross` for eight RIDs into
    `$(OutDir)runtimes/<rid>/native/`, and the .NET host puts that directory on
    NATIVE_DLL_SEARCH_DIRECTORIES — but `Cross.GetApi()` does not go through a `DllImport`, it
    goes through Silk.NET's own `LibraryLoader`, and what that finds is a different set of paths
    on every platform. On the ubuntu leg it finds nothing: six of the nine cases in
    `Vixen.Raven.Transpile.Tests` died inside `Cross.GetApi()` with
    `FileNotFoundException: Could not load from any of the possible library names!`, and read as
    six defects in the shader compiler (#1027).

    ⚠ **The same shape is already written down in `Directory.Packages.props` for Assimp**, and
    measured there: the loader does not look in `runtimes/` on Linux, and the same file placed
    beside the assembly loads. That note calls copying it beside the binary "owed"; this is that,
    for the library whose absence was turning a CI leg red.

    Measured here the other way round, on macOS, which is the leg that works: delete
    `$(OutDir)runtimes/osx-arm64` and the suite reproduces the ubuntu run exactly — 6 skipped,
    3 passed, the two unguarded cases red. Put the identical file flat beside the assembly with
    the RID directory still gone and all eleven pass. So beside-the-assembly is a path this
    loader takes, and it is the one path that does not depend on which RID name the loader
    guesses.

    ⚠ Green on macOS is not green on Linux, and the ubuntu leg is where this has to be judged.
    `VIXEN_REQUIRE_SPIRV_CROSS` is still off there deliberately (see `.github/workflows/ci.yml`),
    so this change can only turn that leg's *skips* into passes and can never turn it red — and
    the skip count is what says whether the copy worked. Flip the promise on once it reads zero.

    `NETCoreSdkPortableRuntimeIdentifier` rather than `RuntimeIdentifier`: none of these projects
    sets one, so the only RID in scope is the machine's own, which is what the SDK reports and
    exactly the native that has to be beside the binary for a run on that machine. A cross-build
    would want the target's instead, and none of these projects does one.
  -->
  <Target Name="VixenFlattenSpirvCrossNative"
          AfterTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(NETCoreSdkPortableRuntimeIdentifier)' != ''">
    <ItemGroup>
      <VixenSpirvCrossNative
        Include="$(OutDir)runtimes/$(NETCoreSdkPortableRuntimeIdentifier)/native/*spirv-cross*"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <Copy SourceFiles="@(VixenSpirvCrossNative)"
          DestinationFolder="$(OutDir)"
          SkipUnchangedFiles="true"
          Condition="'@(VixenSpirvCrossNative)' != ''"/>
  </Target>

</Project>
